%N A397677 Composite integers k whose set of digits is equal to the union of the sets of digits of its divisors.
%C A397677 Each term contains digit 1 since each term has 1 as a divisor.
%H A397677 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A397677/b397677.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A397677 2510 is a term since its divisors are 1, 2, 5, 10, 251, 502, 1255, 2510 => {0,1,2,5}.
%e A397677 16325 is a term since its divisors are 1, 5, 25, 653, 3265, 16325 => {1,2,3,5,6}.
%e A397677 21109 is a term since its divisors are 1, 11, 19, 101, 209, 1111, 1919, 21109 => {0,1,2,9}.
%e A397677 25531 is a term since its divisors are 1, 11, 121, 211, 2321, 25531 => {1, 2, 3, 5}.
%e A397677 72013 is a term since its divisors are 1, 23, 31, 101, 713, 2323, 3131, 72013 => {0,1,2,3,7}.
%o A397677 (Python)
%o A397677 from sympy import divisors, isprime
%o A397677 def ok(n): return n>4 and not isprime(n) and set(str(n)) == set().union(*(set(str(d)) for d in divisors(n)))
%o A397677 print([k for k in range(11400) if ok(k)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Aug 16 2026
%Y A397677 Subset of each of A002808, A011531, A092911, A092912.
